#!/usr/bin/env -S mos build --open

#set document(
  title: "Math via Helvetica",
  author: "Mosaic contributors",
  language: "en",
)

#set page(
  paper: "A4",
  margin: 24mm,
  numbering: "bottom-center",
)

#set text(
  font: "Helvetica",
  size: 11pt,
  leading: 1.35,
)

= You probably want the other example

If you have arrived here looking for math support, please read
the `hello` example first. The bundled Noto Sans family now
resolves math operators (≤ ≥ ≠ √ ∂ ∑ −) automatically through
per-glyph fallback to the embedded Noto Sans Math cut, mid-line,
without `#set text(font: ...)` ceremony. That is the path you
want for almost every document.

This example exists for the *other* case: when you, for some
defensible reason, want to **avoid** embedding font data and let
the reader's built-in Helvetica supply the outlines.

`#set text(font: "Helvetica")` selects that path. The PDF ships
without `/FontFile2` streams, file size drops by roughly 340 KB,
and the reader is asked nicely to render ten specific math
glyphs from its own AGL-named Core 14 metrics.

**Asked nicely** is doing a lot of work in that sentence.

== What you get if you stay on this path

The Adobe Core 14 AFMs name ten math glyphs, accessible through
a per-document `/Differences` array layered on top of
`WinAnsiEncoding`:

1. `lessequal` (≤),
2. `greaterequal` (≥),
3. `notequal` (≠),
4. `partialdiff` (∂),
5. `summation` (∑),
6. `radical` (√),
7. `minus` (−, the real one, U+2212, *not* the ASCII hyphen-minus on your keyboard),
8. `Delta` (∆, the math increment, U+2206; the Greek capital letter delta at U+0394 is a different codepoint and is *not* reachable on this path),
9. `lozenge` (◊),
10. `fraction` (⁄, U+2044, *not* the ASCII slash).

A worked sample, for the snapshot:

Inequalities: a ≤ b, x ≥ y, p ≠ q. Calculus: ∂f/∂x = 0, ∑a = 1,
√n ≥ 0. Geometry: ∆ABC, optional steps marked ◊. The minus
operator (a − b) is U+2212, distinct from the hyphen (a - b);
the fraction slash (1 ⁄ 2) is U+2044, distinct from `/`.

== What you do not get

Everything else. Off the top, and in no particular order: the
infinity symbol, the universal and existential quantifiers, the
element-of relation, nabla, the product operator, the circled
operators (oplus, otimes, and their siblings), sub- and
superscripts, the calligraphic and blackletter alphabets every
paper on algebra leans on, and most Greek letters used as math
variables.

We have deliberately not pasted those codepoints into this
paragraph, because the Base14 path substitutes `?` for anything
outside `WinAnsiEncoding` and the AGL math subset, and a wall
of `?` is not the demo we are going for here. If you would like
to see the substitution live, type one in: ∞ becomes ?, ∀
becomes ?, the Greek letter you wanted becomes ?. There is no
`/Differences` trick that conjures glyphs out of a font that
does not contain them. For any math beyond the ten glyphs
above, switch back to the embedded fallback path.

== The substitution problem

Even within the ten supported glyphs, what the reader actually
paints depends on which font fontconfig hands the reader as its
"Helvetica." Different installs of the same reader can end up
with different substitutes:

- `lozenge` (◊) is the most fragile: many free Helvetica substitutes simply do not contain it, and you get an empty box.
- Adobe Reader has been observed splicing in spacing-substitution composition for `/Differences`-mapped glyphs, most visibly for the Romanian comma-below characters ț and ș.
- Within the same Poppler-based reader family (GNOME Papers, Evince, zathura), different installs can pick different substitutes via fontconfig and end up with different glyph coverage.

If you target a specific reader and rely on any of these glyphs,
render on that reader before you ship. We will not do this for
you. We barely do it for ourselves.
